# Normalize to LF in the repository; let git decide the working-tree ending by platform.
* text=auto

# Golden files are compared byte-for-byte against renderer output, and TemplateRenderer
# normalizes to LF. A clone with core.autocrlf=true (the Git-for-Windows default) would
# otherwise rewrite these to CRLF on checkout and fail the comparison on every line.
*.g.cs.txt text eol=lf

# Scriban templates are rendered verbatim into the golden output, so their line endings
# are part of the generated artifact rather than a local editing preference.
*.scriban text eol=lf

# [lf-everywhere] pinned generated artifacts (above) but never our own source. A C# raw string
# literal's line endings are whatever bytes sit in the .cs file at that point — they are data, not
# editing preference, the same way a Scriban template's are. The first real CI run
# (windows-latest, core.autocrlf=true default) proved it: UpgradeCommandTests.cs wrote
# .config/dotnet-tools.json from a multi-line raw string literal and then asserted on the result
# with a hard-coded "\n" between two of its keys. Locally core.autocrlf=input keeps the checkout
# LF, so the literal carried LF and the assertion passed; on windows-latest the same file checked
# out CRLF, the literal carried CRLF, and the assertion failed — not because behaviour differed,
# but because the checkout did. Confirmed by direct experiment: cloning with
# `git -c core.autocrlf=true clone` reproduced the failure outside CI.
#
# Pinning eol=lf changes nothing already committed — `git show HEAD:<path> | grep -P '\r'` found
# no CRLF in any tracked .cs blob, because `* text=auto` above already normalizes to LF in the
# object database; only checkout was ever platform-dependent. The population of clones this
# affects is days old and small, so this is the cheapest point at which to close the gap. Modern
# .NET tooling does not care: this repository's own tests already build and run under LF .cs files
# on Windows via core.autocrlf=input, which is exactly what this line makes universal.
*.cs text eol=lf
